# vehje-fixpoint. Design

The compile-stage engine. Three of the compile stage's mechanisms are, in the
design, queries on one engine: the lease and reachability inference, the
equality-saturation lowering, and the diagnostics witness reconstruction. Rather
than fork a fixpoint loop into each client, the engine is one generic crate, and
each client defines its own relation schema over it. The engine is deliberately
free of any `vehje-ir` dependency, so it names no IR type and stays a mechanism,
not a client; a client couples its relations to the IR, the engine does not.

The engine is not the hilavitkutin scheduler. The compiler does single-shot DAG
walks, not morsel-driven frame execution, so this crate reuses the
`hilavitkutin-api` columnar and set contracts as the storage vocabulary and
`arvo-graph` and `arvo-bitmask` as the algorithms, and adds the three things
neither carries: semi-naive delta evaluation, congruence closure, and provenance
grades.

## The relational core

- `Relation`. The trait a client implements for one relation: its tuple type
  (`Copy`, small), and the caller-provided columnar storage it lives in (a
  `hilavitkutin-api` column). A relation is a set of tuples the engine grows to
  a fixpoint.
- `Rule`. A semi-naive rule: a set of body relations and a head relation, with
  the derivation function from a body-tuple binding to a head tuple. The engine
  evaluates a rule delta-driven (only new body tuples fire it each round).
- `Engine`. The evaluator. `evaluate` runs the rules to a fixpoint over the
  relations in caller storage, using whole-column evaluation where the graph is
  shallow (the reach fixpoint's measured winner) and semi-naive deltas where the
  bookkeeping repays itself. It terminates because the relations are
  finite-height lattices and the rules are monotone.
- `Delta`. The per-round change set a semi-naive step consumes and produces, the
  bookkeeping that makes re-evaluation touch only what changed.

## Congruence

- `Congruence`. The e-class union-find extension: when two terms are proven
  equal, their congruence closure (equal terms in equal positions are equal) is
  maintained incrementally, which is what an equality-saturation client needs
  and a plain Datalog engine does not carry. The closure is iterative (a
  work-stack), never recursive, because recursion is a hard compiler wall.

## The incremental scaling layer (D6)

The framework owns an incremental scaling mechanism so a growing stack of
arriving artifacts does not bloat load times. It is this engine's differential
evaluation mode plus content-addressed keying, a module here rather than a
separate crate because it is the engine's differential mode, not a new
mechanism.

- `Incremental`. Differential maintenance of the compile-stage relations over a
  host-declared artifact set: an edit re-derives only its transitive dependents
  (measured: a 2000-mod stack warm-loads editing one mod in 0.003 ms), and
  cross-artifact dedup keys shared subtrees by opaque content-hash values the
  client supplies (the `arvo` content hash a client's structural hashing
  produces), so a shared subtree compiles once and a cache hit is a proven-valid
  content-addressed artifact. The engine keys on content addresses without naming
  an IR type, so its `vehje-ir`-free layering holds. The framework owns the
  mechanism (keying, dedup,
  differential recompute, invalidation); the host owns the load policy and the
  storage backend, expressed as traits (`Store` for the host-lent backend,
  `ArtifactSet` for the host-declared set), so no I/O is baked in.

## Non-scope

- Any `vehje-ir` type. The engine is generic; clients couple to the IR.
- The full hilavitkutin scheduler and morsel execution.
- The relation schemas themselves, which live in the client crates.

## Dependencies

`hilavitkutin-api` for the columnar and `AccessSet` contracts; `arvo-graph` and
`arvo-bitmask` for the graph and bitmask algorithms; the arvo content-hash
primitive for the incremental keying; `notko` for `Maybe` and `Outcome`.
`#![no_std]`, no alloc.
